A rigid motion is a rotation and a translation: the pose of a body, or the change of coordinates
between two frames. An extended pose adds a velocity. Both are groups built on the
[rotations](../rotations/), and this note gives them a retraction, their group's maps, and their
exponential. The quaternion product $\otimes$, the covering $\pi$, the exponential $\Exp$ and its
Jacobians $J_l$ and $J_r$ are those of that note, and tangent coordinates are ordered rotation
first.

## The groups

A rigid motion is a pair $(q, p)$ of a unit quaternion and a vector of $\R^3$, with the product

$$
	(q_1, p_1)(q_2, p_2) = \bigl(q_1 \otimes q_2,\ p_1 + \pi(q_1)\, p_2\bigr) , @tag(product)
$$

the identity $(1, 0)$ and the inverse $(\bar q, -\pi(q)\transpose p)$. It acts on a point as $x
\mapsto \pi(q)\, x + p$, so that the product is composition: the motion $(q, p)$ takes coordinates in
the body's frame to coordinates in the outer frame, $p$ is where the body is, and $\pi(q)$ is how
it is turned. This is the semidirect product $S^3 \ltimes \R^3$, and up to the sign of its
quaternion, $(q, p) \mapsto (-q, p)$, it is the group $SE(3)$ of rigid motions, a
[quotient](../quotient-manifold/) as the rotations are. Its dimension is $6$. As a homogeneous
matrix it is

$$
	\begin{bmatrix} \pi(q) & p \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} ,
$$

whose product is [](eq:product) and whose action on $(x, 1)$ is the action on the point $x$.

An *extended pose* is a triple $(q, v, p)$, with the velocity carried as the position is:

$$
	(q_1, v_1, p_1)(q_2, v_2, p_2) = \bigl(q_1 \otimes q_2,\ v_1 + \pi(q_1)\, v_2,\ p_1 + \pi(q_1)\, p_2\bigr) .
$$

Up to sign this is the group $SE_2(3)$, of dimension $9$, the state of a body whose attitude,
velocity and position are estimated together.

## The chart

The groups are not [products](../product-manifold/) of the rotations with a Euclidean space as
groups, though they are as manifolds, and they have a retraction of their own.

<Statement {...charts} />

<Proof>

The product of $(q, p)$ and $(1 \oplus \phi, \rho)$ is $(q \otimes (1 \oplus \phi),\ p + \pi(q)\,
\rho)$, the chart, and likewise for the extended pose; at $(\phi, \rho) = 0$ it is $(q, p)$. Its
derivative there is the identity once the tangent space at $(q, p)$ is identified with $\R^6$ by
the rotation's coordinates and the translation's coordinates in the frame of the point, which is
the identification the left translation carries from the identity. For fixed $(q, p)$ the chart is
the rotation's chart in its first coordinates and the affine map $\rho \mapsto p + \pi(q)\, \rho$ of
$\R^3$ onto itself in the rest, which gives its domain and its inverse. With the Cayley chart,
$(-q) \oplus \phi = -(q \oplus \phi)$ and $q' \ominus q$ is unchanged by either sign, and the rest
reads the quaternion only through $\pi$, with $\pi(-q) = \pi(q)$.

</Proof>

With the Cayley chart, then, this is a retraction on $SE(3)$ and on $SE_2(3)$ themselves, by
[%full](statement:math/quotient-manifold/descent), defined on all of $\R^6$ or $\R^9$ and missing
only the motions whose rotation is a half-turn from the point's.

The translation coordinates are in the frame of the point: a step $\rho$ moves the position along
the body's own axes. That is how the error of an odometer or of a scan matcher is given, and it is
what the group's left translation produces. A pose whose position's error is given in a fixed
frame is a different manifold with a retraction, the product of the rotations with $\R^3$, whose
chart is $(q \oplus \phi,\ p + \rho)$. The two have the same points, and the identity between them
is a change of chart: the product's coordinates $(\phi, \rho)$ reach $(q \oplus \phi,\ p + \rho)$,
whose coordinates in the motion's chart are $(\phi,\ \pi(q)\transpose \rho)$, so the
[derivative in charts](../derivatives-in-charts/) of the identity from the product to the motion
is $\operatorname{diag}(I, \pi(q)\transpose)$.

## The maps of the group

With $R = \pi(q)$, the maps a computation needs of a rigid motion are these.

| map | of | as |
| --- | --- | --- |
| composition | $T_1, T_2$ | $(q_1 \otimes q_2,\ p_1 + R_1 p_2)$ |
| inverse | $T$ | $(\bar q,\ -R\transpose p)$ |
| relative motion | $T_1, T_2$ | $T_1^{-1} T_2 = (\bar q_1 \otimes q_2,\ R_1\transpose (p_2 - p_1))$ |
| action on a point | $T, x$ | $R x + p$ |
| action on a direction | $T, x$ | $R x$ |
| inverse action | $T, x$ | $R\transpose (x - p)$ |

Each is smooth, and each is a map of $SE(3)$: the sign of a quaternion changes at most the sign of
the result's. The relative motion $T_1^{-1} T_2$ is the second pose seen from the first, which is
what an odometer measures between two poses and what a camera measures of a landmark.

The *adjoint* is the conjugation of the group's Lie algebra, $T \Exp(\xi)\, T^{-1} =
\Exp(\Ad(T)\, \xi)$, and carries a perturbation in the frame of the motion to the outer frame,
$T \oplus \xi = (1 \oplus \Ad(T)\, \xi)\, T + O(\lVert \xi \rVert^2)$. In the order $(\phi, \rho)$ it
is

$$
	\Ad(q, p) = \begin{bmatrix} R & 0 \\ [p]_\times R & R \end{bmatrix}, @tag(adjoint)
$$

which many references write with the blocks the other way about, their translation first. Unlike
the rotations' [](eq:math/rotations/conjugation), the relation holds to first order only, since
the chart is not the exponential.

## The exponential and the logarithm

The exponential is the motion at a constant body twist $(\phi, \rho)$, rotating at the rate $\phi$
while translating at the body velocity $\rho$, for unit time, and the logarithm is its inverse:

$$
	\Exp(\phi, \rho) = \bigl(\Exp \phi,\ J_l(\phi)\, \rho\bigr)
$$

and

$$
	\Log(q, p) = \bigl(\phi,\ J_l(\phi)^{-1} p\bigr), \qquad \phi = \Log q, @tag(exponential)
$$

the logarithm defined for $q \ne -1$. A pose moving at constant body rates $(\omega, v)$ for a time
$\Delta t$ moves from $T_k$ to $T_{k+1} = T_k\, \Exp(\omega\, \Delta t,\ v\, \Delta t)$, exactly.
With the rotation vector $\rv$ of the shorter turn in place of $\Log$, the logarithm is a map of
$SE(3)$, discontinuous at the half-turns as on the rotations.

The exponential couples the translation to the rotation through the left Jacobian, and the chart
of [%full](statement:charts) does not. In exponential coordinates a step from $(q, p)$ reaches $(q
\otimes \Exp \phi,\ p + R\, J_l(\phi)\, \rho)$, and

$$
	J_l(\phi)\, \rho = \rho + \tfrac12\, \phi \times \rho + O(\lVert \phi \rVert^2 \lVert \rho \rVert) ,
$$

so the two agree to first order and differ at second order by the term $\tfrac12 \phi \times \rho$.
First derivatives read in the chart are therefore the Jacobians of the textbooks, as on the
rotations ([%full](statement:math/rotations/agreement)); second derivatives are not, and are the
chart's own.

The motions' Jacobians, with $\Exp(\xi + \varepsilon) = \Exp(\mathcal J_l(\xi)\, \varepsilon)\,
\Exp(\xi) = \Exp(\xi)\, \Exp(\mathcal J_r(\xi)\, \varepsilon)$ to first order, are in the order
$(\phi, \rho)$

$$
	\mathcal J_l(\phi, \rho) = \begin{bmatrix} J_l(\phi) & 0 \\ Q(\phi, \rho) & J_l(\phi) \end{bmatrix},
	\qquad
	\mathcal J_r(\phi, \rho) = \mathcal J_l(-\phi, -\rho),
	\qquad
	\mathcal J_l^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} J_l^{-1} & 0 \\ -J_l^{-1} Q\, J_l^{-1} & J_l^{-1} \end{bmatrix}, @tag(jacobians)
$$

with $\Phi = [\phi]_\times$, $\Psi = [\rho]_\times$, $\theta = \lVert \phi \rVert$ and

$$
	Q(\phi, \rho) = \tfrac12 \Psi + B\, (\Phi \Psi + \Psi \Phi + \Phi \Psi \Phi)
	+ D\, (\Phi^2 \Psi + \Psi \Phi^2 - 3\, \Phi \Psi \Phi)
	+ E\, (\Phi \Psi \Phi^2 + \Phi^2 \Psi \Phi) , @tag(q)
$$

where $B$ is the coefficient of [](eq:math/rotations/jacobians) and

$$
	D = \frac{\theta^2 + 2 \cos \theta - 2}{2 \theta^4},
	\qquad
	E = \frac{2\theta - 3 \sin \theta + \theta \cos \theta}{2 \theta^5} .
$$

This is the block of the textbooks written translation first, moved below the diagonal by the
order of the coordinates here. Like the rotations' coefficients, $D$ and $E$ are smooth, with
closed forms that divide by a power of $\theta$.

## Interpolation

Two interpolations between $T_1$ and $T_2$ are in use:

$$
	T_1\, \Exp\bigl(t \Log(T_1^{-1} T_2)\bigr)
	\qquad\text{and}\qquad
	\bigl(q_1 \otimes \Exp(t \Log(\bar q_1 \otimes q_2)),\ (1 - t)\, p_1 + t\, p_2\bigr) . @tag(interpolation)
$$

The first is the screw motion at a constant twist, the one-parameter subgroup through $T_1^{-1}
T_2$ carried to $T_1$. The second interpolates the rotation along its geodesic and the position
along a straight line, the geodesic of the product. Each comes with $\rv$ in place of $\Log$ for
the shorter turn.

## Extended poses

The extended poses have the same maps, with the velocity carried as the position is: the
composition $(q_1 \otimes q_2,\ v_1 + R_1 v_2,\ p_1 + R_1 p_2)$, the inverse $(\bar q,\ -R\transpose
v,\ -R\transpose p)$, the relative motion likewise, the exponential $(\Exp \phi,\ J_l(\phi)\,
\eta,\ J_l(\phi)\, \rho)$ and its logarithm, the left Jacobian with the blocks $J_l(\phi)$ on its
diagonal and $Q(\phi, \eta)$ and $Q(\phi, \rho)$ below it in its first column, and in the order
$(\phi, \eta, \rho)$ the adjoint

$$
	\Ad(q, v, p) = \begin{bmatrix} R & 0 & 0 \\ [v]_\times R & R & 0 \\ [p]_\times R & 0 & R \end{bmatrix} . @tag(extended-adjoint)
$$

Their action on points is that of their pose $(q, p)$. As a matrix an extended pose is the $5
\times 5$ one with the columns $(R, v, p)$ over the rows $(0, 1, 0)$ and $(0, 0, 1)$.
